#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5256297-c928-54e3-bf4d-35f5734f5632" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Surface Integrals Over Parametric Surfaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4518e3d7-a24f-5ca6-a02e-940fc55f5343" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({u}^{2}-{v}^{2}-2u)\\sqrt{8}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351080} {:answer/id #uuid "e2069f29-4d51-5f60-a9dd-fafb3f575ee1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\sqrt{8}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351081} {:answer/id #uuid "33a00a0e-ab3b-5331-9800-ea0eae6e2581" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+u)\\sqrt{8v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351082} {:answer/id #uuid "16a98401-3809-5ca7-b405-9897bc9b31cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({v}^{2}-{u}^{2}-2)\\sqrt{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351083} {:answer/id #uuid "55a55a49-a53d-5f4f-8006-c92711673a33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({u}^{2}+2v+2u)\\sqrt{8}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351084} {:answer/id #uuid "014fc7ab-4b27-5855-99e0-5a6eca199801" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({u}^{2}-{v}^{2}-2u)\\sqrt{8}|v|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "014fc7ab-4b27-5855-99e0-5a6eca199801" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({u}^{2}-{v}^{2}-2u)\\sqrt{8}|v|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351169} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a33f854-4bfe-5310-8686-d5eed1146d88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "60828e8e-d700-5ec8-b0aa-d96b2e205b74" :question/math-academy-id "q-161286" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=-x-y-z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,{v}^{2}-{u}^{2},\\,u⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ Use the scalar surface-area factor $\\|r_u\\times r_v\\|$ with its absolute values for the stated parameter domain. What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a scalar surface integral, the pullback integrand is $f(r(u,v))\\|r_u\\times r_v\\|$. The derivatives and cross product are\n\n$$r_u=(1,-2u,1),\\qquad r_v=(0,2v,0),\\qquad r_u\\times r_v=(-2v,0,2v).$$\n\nThus the nonnegative area factor is\n\n$$\\|r_u\\times r_v\\|=\\sqrt{(-2v)^2+(2v)^2}=\\sqrt8\\,|v|.$$\n\nSubstitution into the scalar function gives\n\n$$f(r(u,v))=-u-(v^2-u^2)-u=u^2-v^2-2u.$$\n\nThe missing integrand for the stated general parameter domain is therefore\n\n$$(u^2-v^2-2u)\\sqrt8\\,|v|.$$\n\nThe source omitted the absolute value and supplied no sign restriction on $v$. Its option $(u^2-v^2-2u)\\sqrt8\\,v$ is valid on a positive-$v$ patch, but fails when $v<0$: at $(u,v)=(1,-1)$ the corrected value is $-2\\sqrt8$, while that source option gives $2\\sqrt8$. The capture used a distinct explicit $v>0$, regular, one-to-one recovery request. This study question retains the original domain and supplies the correct absolute-value choice.\n\nThe parametrization loses rank at $v=0$. As usual, interpreting the displayed equality as an integral over the surface assumes regular patches and no multiple coverage except on negligible boundaries; the local area factor remains $\\sqrt8|v|$."}]}
